MATLAB is a powerful tool that can be used to create intricate and complex abstract art. There are several ways in which MATLAB can be used to generate abstract visuals, including fractal algorithms, noise functions, and mathematical transformations. 
Here is an example code that employs sophisticated algorithms for generating abstract visuals with both haphazard and systematic alterations in form, dimension, and chroma:
```
% Set up the canvas
canvas = zeros(500, 500, 3);
% Generate random noise
noise = randn(500);
% Apply a fractal algorithm to the noise
fractal = fractalnoise(noise, 5);
% Apply a mathematical transformation to the fractal
transformed = imrotate(fractal, 45);
% Apply a color map to the transformed image
colormap = hsv(256);
colormap = colormap(1:200, :);
% Normalize the transformed image
normalized = mat2gray(transformed);
% Apply the colormap to the normalized image
colored = ind2rgb(im2uint8(normalized), colormap);
% Add the colored image to the canvas
canvas = canvas + colored;
% Display the final image
imshow(canvas);
```
This code generates a fractal noise pattern, applies a mathematical transformation to it, and then applies a color map to the transformed image. The resulting image is then added to a canvas to create the final artwork.
To manipulate the chromatic and geometric aspects of the components, you can experiment with different color maps and mathematical transformations. For example, you could try using a different color map to create a different color scheme, or you could apply a different mathematical transformation to create a different geometric pattern.
It is definitely feasible to combine these techniques to produce more elaborate artwork. By experimenting with different algorithms and techniques, you can create complex and visually stunning abstract art.

