Certainly! Here's a sample Java code that can extract data from a deeply nested tag in an XML file using recursion:
```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.parsers.ParserConfigurationException;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.Element;
import org.w3c.dom.Node;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;
import org.xml.sax.SAXException;
public class XMLParser {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        File file = new File("example.xml");
        DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        DocumentBuilder db;
        try {
            db = dbf.newDocumentBuilder();
            Document doc = db.parse(file);
            doc.getDocumentElement().normalize();
            Node root = doc.getDocumentElement();
            List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
            getNodeValue("needle", root, result);
            System.out.println(result);
        } catch (ParserConfigurationException | SAXException | IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
    private static void getNodeValue(String tag, Node node, List<String> result) {
        if (node.getNodeType() == Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {
            Element element = (Element) node;
            if (element.getTagName().equals(tag)) {
                result.add(element.getTextContent());
            }
            NodeList nodeList = element.getChildNodes();
            for (int i = 0; i < nodeList.getLength(); i++) {
                getNodeValue(tag, nodeList.item(i), result);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we first create a `File` object that represents the XML file we want to parse. We then create a `DocumentBuilderFactory` object and use it to create a `DocumentBuilder` object. We use this `DocumentBuilder` object to parse the XML file and create a `Document` object.
Next, we normalize the `Document` object and get the root element of the XML file. We also create an empty `List` object to store the results of our search.
We then call the `getNodeValue` method, passing in the tag we want to search for, the root element, and the result list. This method uses recursion to traverse the XML file and find all occurrences of the tag we are searching for. If it finds a matching tag, it adds the text content of that tag to the result list.
Finally, we print out the result list to the console.
Note that this code assumes that the XML file is well-formed and does not contain any errors. It also assumes that the tag we are searching for is unique within the XML file. If the tag appears multiple times, the code will return all occurrences of the tag.

