To implement a binary search algorithm in C++ while adhering to formal and professional writing standards, you can follow these guidelines:
1. Begin with a clear and concise problem statement that describes the purpose of the algorithm and what it does.
2. Use proper indentation and spacing to make your code easy to read and understand.
3. Use meaningful variable names that reflect the purpose of the variable.
4. Add comments to explain what the code is doing, especially for complex or non-obvious parts of the algorithm.
5. Use appropriate data types and functions to ensure the algorithm is efficient and effective.
Here is an example of a binary search algorithm implemented in C++ that follows these guidelines:
```c++
/**
 * @brief Binary search algorithm to search for a value in a sorted array.
 * @param arr The sorted array to search.
 * @param l The left index of the subarray to search.
 * @param r The right index of the subarray to search.
 * @param x The value to search for.
 * @return The index of the value if found, or -1 if not present in the array.
 */
int binarySearch(int arr[], int l, int r, int x) {
    // Check if there are still elements to search
    if (r >= l) {
        // Calculate the middle index of the subarray
        int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;
        // If the middle element is the value we're looking for, return its index
        if (arr[mid] == x) {
            return mid;
        }
        // If the middle element is greater than the value we're looking for,
        // search the left half of the subarray
        if (arr[mid] > x) {
            return binarySearch(arr, l, mid - 1, x);
        }
        // Otherwise, search the right half of the subarray
        return binarySearch(arr, mid + 1, r, x);
    }
    // If the value is not found, return -1
    return -1;
}
```
The binary search algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half until the target value is found or the subarray becomes empty. This approach is more efficient than a linear search, which would require checking every element in the array until the target value is found. By dividing the search interval in half each time, the binary search algorithm can quickly narrow down the search space and find the target value in O(log n) time, where n is the number of elements in the array.

