Ensuring equal representation and fair treatment of all genders in the workplace involves a combination of policies, practices, and cultural changes. While a C++ program cannot directly address all of these aspects, it can be used to support certain policies and practices. One example of this is using a program to anonymize resumes during the recruitment process to remove any potential gender bias.
Here is an example code that demonstrates how to anonymize resumes using C++:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    // Open input and output files
    ifstream inFile("input.txt");
    ofstream outFile("output.txt");
    // Read each line of the input file
    string line;
    while (getline(inFile, line)) {
        // Convert the line to lowercase
        transform(line.begin(), line.end(), line.begin(), ::tolower);
        // Replace any gender-specific words with neutral terms
        line.replace(line.find("mr."), 3, "mx.");
        line.replace(line.find("mrs."), 4, "mx.");
        line.replace(line.find("miss"), 4, "mx.");
        line.replace(line.find("ms."), 3, "mx.");
        line.replace(line.find("he"), 2, "they");
        line.replace(line.find("him"), 3, "them");
        line.replace(line.find("his"), 3, "their");
        // Write the anonymized line to the output file
        outFile << line << endl;
    }
    // Close the input and output files
    inFile.close();
    outFile.close();
    return 0;
}
```
This program reads in a file called "input.txt" that contains resumes and writes an anonymized version of each resume to a new file called "output.txt". The program converts each resume to lowercase and replaces any gender-specific words with neutral terms. For example, "Mr. John Smith" is replaced with "Mx. John Smith", and "he" is replaced with "they".
While this program may not solve all issues related to gender bias in the workplace, it can help to reduce the impact of unconscious bias during the recruitment process.

